<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/2nd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Apamea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/188-bce">188 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-seleucid-war">Roman–Seleucid War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Apamea was the peace settlement of 188 BCE between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> after his defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magnesia">Battle of Magnesia</a>. It stripped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> of their Anatolian territories beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a>, imposed 15,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> in war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>, and forbade them from keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> and a substantial fleet. Accounts of the Seleucid Empire treat it as a decisive blow to Seleucid power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-apamea.jpg" alt="Treaty of Apamea" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kryston · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treaty_of_Apamea.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Treaty of Apamea was the peace treaty made in 188 BCE between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>, ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>. It ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-seleucid-war">Roman–Seleucid War</a>. It followed Roman victories at the Battle of Thermopylae in 191 BCE and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magnesia">Battle of Magnesia</a> in 190 BCE, and Roman and Rhodian naval victories over the Seleucid fleet. It was formalised at Apamea in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>According to Appian, the treaty obliged Antiochus to give up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> altogether and all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a>. He had to surrender all his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> and keep none in future. His navy was limited to twelve warships for keeping his subjects under control, although he could build more if attacked. He could not recruit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> north of the Taurus, which was territory just ceded to the Roman allies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, nor receive fugitives from it. He had to give twenty hostages chosen by the Roman consul, changed every third year except for the son of Antiochus.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> were to pay an indemnity of 15,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>: 500 Euboic talents at once, 2,500 more when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a> ratified the treaty, and instalments of 1,000 talents each year for twelve years. They also owed 540,000 modii of corn. Antiochus had to give up all prisoners and deserters to his enemies, and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> whatever remained of the possessions Antiochus had gained by his agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-i">Attalus I</a>, the father of Eumenes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> gave control of a large part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> to Eumenes. Antiochus kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>, while most of Lycia and Caria became part of the Rhodian Peraia. The treaty let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> extend their political hegemony to the Eastern Mediterranean, although their power was still indirect and relied on allies of the second rank such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>. The harsh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> weakened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> by causing a shortage of money. The naval conditions appear to have fallen into abeyance, but the other conditions held.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> used the threat of renewed war to check Seleucid power. In the Sixth Syrian War it insisted that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> leave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Empire</a> alone after they had occupied much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> in 168 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes">Antiochus IV</a> grudgingly accepted. As late as 162 BCE, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, a Roman delegation under Gnaeus Octavius used the treaty to hamstring Seleucid war elephants and destroy ships. Leptines of Laodicea killed Octavius, and the Senate seems to have disavowed Octavius's actions and let the killer go.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Apamea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Apamea</b> in 188 BCE gave it to the Kingdom of Pergamon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Apamea</b> in 188 BCE added Lycia and much of Caria, which Rhodes ruled as subject lands until Rome took them away in 167 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Seleucid Empire declined in stages over some 125 years, from the <b>Peace of Apamea</b> in 188 BCE to Pompey's annexation of Syria in 64–63 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Loyal alliance with Rome brought Eumenes II wide lands in Asia Minor after the <b>Peace of Apamea</b> in 188 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-pergamon">Kingdom of Pergamon</a></li></ul></section>
